  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two kinds of shape-morphing devices and the applications they enable. In the top row, a simple flat pattern is shown transforming into another defined shape when exposed to external stimuli such as temperature, light, magnetic fields, or pneumatic actuation. The idea is pattern-to-pattern conversion: a structure starts flat and then switches into a specific curved form. This stimulus-driven morphing is linked to uses in untethered soft robots, medical or diagnostic robots inside the body, cell-related experiments using shaped surfaces, and deployable aerospace components.\n\nThe bottom row shows programmable shape-morphing devices. Here, a tiled or pixelated surface is controlled by discrete or continuous signals, allowing a wider range of target shapes rather than a single preset transformation. The examples suggest dynamically reconfigurable surfaces that can produce different curvatures and textures on demand. These programmable systems are associated with tactile or haptic interfaces, tangible user interfaces for AR/VR, and optical or acoustic metamaterials whose surface geometry can tune how waves interact with them.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes the shift from passive stimulus-responsive shape change to actively programmable, multifunctional morphing platforms.",
